TMOG-11 ON THE PDP -- updated 2026-09-22

The homepage and the live TMOG-11 frame are served by the real PDP-11/83.
No kernel, inetd configuration, httpd binary, or login accounts were changed.

FILES
  /usr/src/local/webtop/webtop.c       privileged sampler source
  /usr/src/local/webtop/webtop-fields.h integer CPU and display helpers
  /usr/src/local/webtop/webtop-cgi.c   fixed unprivileged CGI launcher
  /usr/local/libexec/webtop           root-owned sampler, mode 4711
  /home/www/cgi-bin/webtop            root-owned CGI launcher, mode 755
  /home/www/index.html                homepage
  /home/www/index.html.bak            exact original homepage backup
  /home/www/index.previous.html       viewable copy of the old homepage
  /home/www/pdp11.jpg.bak             original photograph backup
  /tmp/webtop.cache                   root-owned frame, mode 644
  /tmp/webtop.cache.new               exclusive temporary output
  /tmp/webtop.lock                    root-owned persistent flock file, 600

BUILD (on this machine)
  cd /usr/src/local/webtop
  cc -O -i -o webtop webtop.c
  cc -O -o webtop-cgi webtop-cgi.c

The -i flag gives code and data separate PDP-11 address spaces.
No curses or extra libraries. All sampler arithmetic/formatting is integer.
Kernel sampling follows /usr/src/ucb/top/top.c (Digby Tarvin and later
contributors); the local /unix symbols and native headers are used.
The native sysctl returns a byte count on success, not necessarily zero.

INSTALL / UPDATE (root)
  cp webtop /usr/local/libexec/webtop.new
  chown root /usr/local/libexec/webtop.new
  chmod 4711 /usr/local/libexec/webtop.new
  mv -f /usr/local/libexec/webtop.new /usr/local/libexec/webtop
  cp webtop-cgi /home/www/cgi-bin/webtop.new
  chown root /home/www/cgi-bin/webtop.new
  chmod 755 /home/www/cgi-bin/webtop.new
  mv /home/www/cgi-bin/webtop.new /home/www/cgi-bin/webtop

HTTP
  Existing server: /usr/libexec/httpd
  Existing source: /home/user/src/httpd/httpd.c
  Existing inetd service user: www (uid 80)
  Document root: /home/www
  Endpoint: /cgi-bin/webtop

This httpd refuses setuid CGI binaries and passes no CGI environment.
The ordinary launcher execs one fixed setuid helper outside the document
root, with no user arguments and a fixed minimal environment. The helper
writes the full HTTP/1.0 status line and LF-separated headers expected by
this server. It does not read a query string or request body. This server
includes query strings in its filesystem lookup, so use the exact URL.

SAMPLING / CACHE
A new frame uses two samples with a one-second sleep between them. It shows
up to 22 processes, sorted by interval CPU percentage, then total CPU time.
Commands are short names
from u_comm, never process arguments or environment. Process user-area
reads are checked against u_procp, PID, process state, address, and SLOAD.
User data races are marked [unavailable]. Uptime and clock use UTC.
RAM, swap and per-process memory use KiB. The process table is at most 79
columns wide, including all 16 command-name characters. Per-row K suffixes
are omitted; the footer identifies the units. TIME is cumulative CPU seconds,
with an h suffix for whole hours once the seconds field would overflow.
CPU% replaces DTIME: user+system CPU ticks divided by each process's measured
wall-clock interval, rounded to ticks using the native hz. This includes
sample-sweep overhead, not just the sleep. New/reused PIDs, unreadable user
areas, zombies and invalid clock/counter intervals show '-' rather than a
false zero. Sorting puts unavailable CPU values after measured values.
The overall CPU counters bracket both sampling sweeps and the sleep; the
header reports that measured interval. Individual process reads span slightly
different windows, so their percentages need not sum exactly to the summary.
M is C for SLOAD (in core/RAM), S for swapped, or '-' for zombies. It is a
residency flag, not an RSS estimate. TTY uses u_ttyp/u_ttyd and a bounded scan
of /dev character-device names; devices are never opened. The tty prefix is
omitted (ttyp0 becomes p0), console is cons, '-' means no controlling terminal,
and '?' means unavailable. At most 512 directory entries and 64 terminal
names are examined/stored, once per new frame after sampling.
The process arrays are capped at 36,000 bytes total and 256 slots; a cap
notice is displayed if applicable. Map buffers are capped at 4,096 bytes.

All callers, including CLI, share the cache. Frames younger than five
seconds are reused. Exactly one stale request holds an exclusive nonblocking
flock; other requests receive the previous complete frame. New frames are
written to an exclusively created file, then atomically renamed. There is
no daemon and no work when nobody requests a snapshot.

Unlike an expiring O_EXCL lock, flock releases automatically on exit,
crash, or the 12-second sampler timeout. The lock FILE intentionally remains;
do not delete it while requests are active. Failed attempts are throttled
for five seconds as well. A root-owned sticky /tmp and regular, root-owned,
single-link cache/lock files are required. Symlinks and writable impostor
files are rejected. The setuid helper clears its environment, disables core
dumps, uses only fixed paths, and never launches a shell or another program.

The page requests the same-origin endpoint no faster than once per five
seconds, avoids overlapping requests, pauses in hidden tabs, and provides
a Pause button. An error leaves the previous snapshot visible. The age
includes the server-reported cache age. Responses are marked no-store.

TEST
  make test-fields
  /usr/local/libexec/webtop
  /usr/local/libexec/webtop | wc
  From the LAN: http://192.168.1.29/
  From the LAN: http://192.168.1.29/cgi-bin/webtop

DISABLE LIVE SAMPLING (root)
  chmod 700 /usr/local/libexec/webtop
The webpage retains the old frame or displays its connection error.

RESTORE THE PREVIOUS HOMEPAGE (root)
  cp -p /home/www/index.html.bak /home/www/index.html
  chmod 644 /home/www/index.html
  chmod 700 /usr/local/libexec/webtop
No inetd restart is needed.

PUBLIC NETWORK / GUEST NOTE
At installation, pdp1173.com resolved to Cloudflare and HTTP returned 404;
its public telnet port timed out. The LAN HTTP and authenticated root FTP
and telnet connections worked. There was no guest entry in /etc/passwd,
and a passwordless guest login was rejected before deployment. Public
gateway/DNS and guest accounts were left as found. The previous homepage
claimed 4 MB; this running kernel reports 3,145,728 bytes (3 MiB). The new
page uses measured RAM in the live listing rather than repeating 4 MB.

VISITOR COUNTER (2026-09-19)
The masthead now displays VISITORS: n in place of THE MACHINE.
See README.visitors for counter paths, session semantics and persistence.

AUTOMATIC FAILOVER
The proxy prefers .29, falls back to .26, and identifies the serving PDP in
the panel. The public visitor total is on caddy and shared by both machines.
See config/README.varnish.md and README.visitors for restoration instructions.
